## Solution

Define the operation: from a number $n$, compute $n^2$, take the sum of its digits $S(n^2)$, and produce the new number $S(n^2)+1$.

Compute the sequence starting from $7$:

| Step | Number | Square | Digit sum | New number |
|------|--------|--------|-----------|------------|
| 0 | 7 | 49 | 13 | 14 |
| 1 | 14 | 196 | 16 | 17 |
| 2 | 17 | 289 | 19 | 20 |
| 3 | 20 | 400 | 4 | 5 |
| 4 | 5 | 25 | 7 | 8 |
| 5 | 8 | 64 | 10 | 11 |
| 6 | 11 | 121 | 4 | 5 |
| 7 | 5 | 25 | 7 | 8 |

From step 4 onward, the sequence enters a cycle of length 3:

$$5 \to 8 \to 11 \to 5 \to 8 \to 11 \to \cdots$$

So for every step $n \ge 4$, the value is determined by $(n-4) \bmod 3$:
- $(n-4) \bmod 3 = 0 \Rightarrow 5$
- $(n-4) \bmod 3 = 1 \Rightarrow 8$
- $(n-4) \bmod 3 = 2 \Rightarrow 11$

For step $1999$:

$$1999 - 4 = 1995, \qquad 1995 = 665 \times 3, \qquad 1995 \bmod 3 = 0.$$

Therefore the value at step $1999$ equals the value at step $4$, which is $\boxed{5}$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
